.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Peter Debye (1912) explained why some gases have a dielectric constant that falls with temperature: their molecules carry a permanent electric dipole moment \boldsymbol\mu = \sum_i q_i \mathbf r_i, which thermal motion partly disorders. The measured moment tests a proposed shape. Water&#x27;s 1.85 D rules out a linear H-O-H, while carbon dioxide&#x27;s zero moment requires a linear O=C=O.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/structure/dipole/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_debye_dipole_moments_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/dipole/plot_01_debye_dipole_moments`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Debye's permanent dipole moments: polar and nonpolar molecules from geometry</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/structure/dipole/plot_01_debye_dipole_moments`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Builds four molecules spanning the five idealized electron-domain polyhedra -- methane (AX4E0, tetrahedral), water (AX2E2, bent from a tetrahedral parent), sulfur tetrafluoride (AX4E1, seesaw from a trigonal bipyramidal parent), and xenon tetrafluoride (AX4E2, square planar from an octahedral parent) -- via build_vsepr_molecule, and verifies the exact idealized bond angles each shape predicts.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/structure/vsepr/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_vsepr_geometries_thumb.png
